If $f$ is not continuous at $x = a$, we say $f$ has a \textbf{discontinuity} at $a$. There are three main types:

\textbf{Type 1: Removable Discontinuity}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a} f(x)$ exists, but either $f(a)$ is undefined or $f(a) \neq \lim_{x \to a} f(x)$
    \item Can be "fixed" by redefining $f(a)$
    \item Example: $f(x) = \frac{x^2 - 4}{x - 2}$ at $x = 2$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Type 2: Jump Discontinuity}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Left and right limits exist but are not equal: $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a^-} f(x) \neq \lim_{x \to a^+} f(x)$
    \item Graph has a "jump" at $x = a$
    \item Example: Step functions, piecewise functions
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Type 3: Infinite Discontinuity}
\begin{itemize}
    \item At least one of the one-sided limits is infinite
    \item Vertical asymptote at $x = a$
    \item Example: $f(x) = \frac{1}{x - 2}$ at $x = 2$
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Determine whether $f(x) = \frac{x^2 - 9}{x - 3}$ is continuous at $x = 3$. If not, what type of discontinuity exists?

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Check if $f(3)$ exists
\[
f(3) = \frac{3^2 - 9}{3 - 3} = \frac{0}{0} \quad \text{(undefined)}
\]

Condition 1 fails! \textcolor{red}{$\times$}

Already we know the function is NOT continuous at $x = 3$.

\textbf{Step 2:} Find $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 3} f(x)$

Factor:
\begin{align*}
f(x) &= \frac{x^2 - 9}{x - 3} \\
&= \frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x - 3} \\
&= x + 3 \quad \text{(for } x \neq 3\text{)}
\end{align*}

Therefore:
\[
\lim_{x \to 3} f(x) = \lim_{x \to 3} (x + 3) = 6
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Classify the discontinuity

Since $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 3} f(x) = 6$ exists (finite), but $f(3)$ is undefined, this is a \textbf{removable discontinuity}.

\textbf{Answer:} The function is \textbf{NOT continuous} at $x = 3$. It has a \textbf{removable discontinuity} (a hole in the graph at the point $(3, 6)$).

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{How to Fix It:}} Define $g(x)$ as:
\[
g(x) = \begin{cases}
\frac{x^2 - 9}{x - 3}, & x \neq 3 \\
6, & x = 3
\end{cases}
\]
Then $g(x)$ is continuous everywhere! \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Define $g(x)$ in a way that extends $g(x) = \frac{x^2 - 9}{x - 3}$ to be continuous at $x = 3$.

(From 2023 past paper, Q1 part vi and 2024 past paper, Q1 part xii)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find the limit at $x = 3$

From the previous example, we found:
\[
\lim_{x \to 3} \frac{x^2 - 9}{x - 3} = 6
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Define the continuous extension

For $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x = 3$, we need:
\[
g(3) = \lim_{x \to 3} g(x) = 6
\]

\textbf{Answer:} Define:
\[
\boxed{g(x) = \begin{cases}
\frac{x^2 - 9}{x - 3}, & x \neq 3 \\
6, & x = 3
\end{cases}}
\]

Or equivalently: $\boxed{g(x) = x + 3}$ for all $x$ (since this equals $\frac{x^2-9}{x-3}$ for $x \neq 3$).

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $g(3) = 6$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
    \item $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 3} g(x) = 6$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
    \item $g(3) = \lim_{x \to 3} g(x)$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\end{itemize}

All three conditions for continuity are satisfied!

\vspace{0.5cm}

A function $f$ is \textbf{continuous on an interval} if it is continuous at every point in that interval.

\textbf{Continuous on $(a, b)$:} Continuous at every point $x$ where $a < x < b$

\textbf{Continuous on $[a, b]$:} Continuous at every point in $(a, b)$, and:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a^+} f(x) = f(a)$ (continuous from the right at $a$)
    \item $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to b^-} f(x) = f(b)$ (continuous from the left at $b$)
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Important Continuous Functions:}}

The following types of functions are continuous on their domains:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Polynomials: continuous everywhere ($\mathbb{R}$)
    \item Rational functions: continuous wherever denominator $\neq 0$
    \item Trigonometric functions: $\sin x$, $\cos x$ continuous on $\mathbb{R}$; $\tan x$ discontinuous at $x = \frac{\pi}{2} + n\pi$
    \item Exponential functions: $e^x$, $a^x$ continuous on $\mathbb{R}$
    \item Logarithmic functions: $\ln x$ continuous on $(0, \infty)$
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} At what points is the function $f(x) = \frac{1}{x^2 - 3x}$ continuous?

(From 2023 past paper, Q1 part vii)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Identify where the function is defined

The function is undefined where the denominator equals zero:
\begin{align*}
x^2 - 3x &= 0 \\
x(x - 3) &= 0 \\
x = 0 \quad &\text{or} \quad x = 3
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Determine continuity

Since $f$ is a rational function, it is continuous wherever it is defined.

The function is defined for all $x$ except $x = 0$ and $x = 3$.

\textbf{Answer:} The function is continuous on:
\[
\boxed{(-\infty, 0) \cup (0, 3) \cup (3, \infty)}
\]

Or equivalently: \boxed{\mathbb{R} \setminus \{0, 3\}}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Note:}} At $x = 0$ and $x = 3$, the function has \textbf{infinite discontinuities} (vertical asymptotes).

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} If $f$ and $g$ are continuous at $a$, prove that $fg$ is also continuous at $a$.

(From 2022 past paper, Q1 part iii)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

To prove $fg$ is continuous at $a$, we must show:
\[
\lim_{x \to a} [f(x) \cdot g(x)] = f(a) \cdot g(a)
\]

\textbf{Given Information:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $f$ is continuous at $a$, so $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = f(a)$
    \item $g$ is continuous at $a$, so $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a} g(x) = g(a)$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Proof:}

Using the Product Rule for Limits:
\begin{align*}
\lim_{x \to a} [f(x) \cdot g(x)] &= \left[\lim_{x \to a} f(x)\right] \cdot \left[\lim_{x \to a} g(x)\right] \\
&= f(a) \cdot g(a)
\end{align*}

Since $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a} [f(x) \cdot g(x)] = f(a) \cdot g(a)$, the product $fg$ is continuous at $a$.

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} $fg$ is continuous at $a$. \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Answer:} Proven. \quad $\boxed{Q.E.D.}$

