Let $f$ be a function defined on an open interval containing $a$ (except possibly at $a$ itself). We say:
\[
\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L
\]

if for every $\varepsilon > 0$ (no matter how small), there exists a $\delta > 0$ such that:
\[
\text{if } 0 < |x - a| < \delta, \text{ then } |f(x) - L| < \varepsilon
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{In Words:}}

We can make $f(x)$ arbitrarily close to $L$ (within $\varepsilon$) by taking $x$ sufficiently close to $a$ (within $\delta$), but not equal to $a$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Understanding the Components:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\varepsilon$ (epsilon): Represents how close we want $f(x)$ to be to $L$ (precision requirement)
    \item $\delta$ (delta): Represents how close $x$ must be to $a$ to achieve that precision
    \item $|x - a|$: Distance from $x$ to $a$ (in the domain)
    \item $|f(x) - L|$: Distance from $f(x)$ to $L$ (in the range)
    \item $0 < |x - a|$: Ensures $x \neq a$ (we approach but don't reach $a$)
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Key Insight:}}

For ANY challenge $\varepsilon > 0$ (no matter how tiny), we must find a response $\delta > 0$ that works. This is a "for all $\varepsilon$, there exists $\delta$" statement.

\vspace{0.5cm}

We say:
\[
\lim_{x \to \infty} f(x) = L
\]

if for every $\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a number $N$ such that:
\[
\text{if } x > N, \text{ then } |f(x) - L| < \varepsilon
\]

\textbf{Similarly, for infinite limits:}

We say:
\[
\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = \infty
\]

if for every $M > 0$, there exists a $\delta > 0$ such that:
\[
\text{if } 0 < |x - a| < \delta, \text{ then } f(x) > M
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Interpretation:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item For $x \to \infty$: We need $x$ large enough (beyond $N$)
    \item For limit = $\infty$: We need $f(x)$ arbitrarily large (exceeding any $M$)
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L$ and $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = M$, then $L = M$.

\textbf{In words:} A function cannot have two different limits at the same point. The limit, if it exists, is unique.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Show that $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{1}{x}$ does not exist.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

We show the limit doesn't exist by demonstrating that one-sided limits are different (one is $+\infty$, the other is $-\infty$).

\textbf{Approach 1: Using One-Sided Limits}

\textbf{Right-hand limit:}
As $x \to 0^+$, $x > 0$, so $\frac{1}{x} \to +\infty$.

For any $M > 0$, choose $\delta = \frac{1}{M}$. If $0 < x < \delta$, then:
\[
\frac{1}{x} > \frac{1}{\delta} = M
\]

So $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0^+} \frac{1}{x} = +\infty$.

\textbf{Left-hand limit:}
As $x \to 0^-$, $x < 0$, so $\frac{1}{x} \to -\infty$.

For any $M > 0$, choose $\delta = \frac{1}{M}$. If $-\delta < x < 0$, then:
\[
\frac{1}{x} < -\frac{1}{\delta} = -M
\]

So $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0^-} \frac{1}{x} = -\infty$.

\textcolor{red}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} Since the one-sided limits are different ($+\infty \neq -\infty$), the two-sided limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{1}{x}$ does not exist.

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\text{Limit does not exist}}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Note:}} Even though we say "the limit is $\pm\infty$," technically this means the limit doesn't exist in the finite sense.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 1.7 Summary}}

\textbf{Epsilon-Delta Definition:}
\[
\boxed{\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L \iff \forall \varepsilon > 0, \exists \delta > 0 : 0 < |x - a| < \delta \implies |f(x) - L| < \varepsilon}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Proof Strategy (Epsilon-Delta):}}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Scratch work:} Start with $|f(x) - L| < \varepsilon$ and work backwards to find what $\delta$ should be
    \item \textbf{Restriction:} For polynomials/complicated functions, first restrict $\delta \leq 1$ to bound terms
    \item \textbf{Formal proof:} Write forwards:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item "Let $\varepsilon > 0$ be given"
        \item "Choose $\delta = ...$"
        \item "Suppose $0 < |x - a| < \delta$"
        \item Show $|f(x) - L| < \varepsilon$
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Common Delta Choices:}}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{5cm}|p{4cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Function Type} & \textbf{Typical $\delta$} \\
\hline
Linear: $mx + b$ & $\delta = \frac{\varepsilon}{|m|}$ \\
\hline
Quadratic: $x^2$ & $\delta = \min\left\{1, \frac{\varepsilon}{2a+1}\right\}$ \\
\hline
Rational: $\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}$ & Restrict first, then find $\delta$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Important Points:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The epsilon-delta definition is the rigorous foundation of calculus
    \item You don't need to find the "best" $\delta$, just one that works
    \item $\delta$ depends on both $\varepsilon$ and $a$
    \item Limits are unique (if they exist)
    \item This definition extends to limits at infinity and infinite limits
\end{itemize}

